331246" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/c3d512c9e89079d86c29d1aaa8feb9fa8a47345fda4a1c0e2f2f5228246d0ef5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t+2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331246/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f7a33f8-b0f6-5265-9d3e-c1ca34c07161"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331246"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-331249"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/bcf4076849dd96858c586f8696414789b3f68074b87652601245407142f2b8fc.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we scale $y=u(t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-331247/selection/d0651a03f89efc45286c5ee45fac8bf8f6d7b9b0cd6a947e1d978a610d619c14" :answer/id #uuid "cb65caf9-11ee-5102-bd8c-b778b8448d0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t+1)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331247/selection/d4411b0b8adc92f9440ec8b8964b562b4b02b35837ef02493727c43f1c036e13" :answer/id #uuid "5dd4508c-f2b4-5f20-85ff-36d928ddf3e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331247/selection/5c02fe71dfac7f98b265c90f1e44aeb5a5c279dcfb3ac6a58ac89e9bcb719224" :answer/id #uuid "6e917be3-f3e8-54e6-8306-847b4db3dbfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t-1)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331247/selection/860efc4fc7ca904e76025a2ad9def958d3f156d5a46bfc9b4fb739fb4c332b9f" :answer/id #uuid "116519e6-e346-5023-94cc-1292281c8a36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331247/selection/829b3930583e141a0ce52144501442c6c6de79c395438fc37ac1abec2dcd19ff" :answer/id #uuid "1eef58be-0a7b-5354-97d0-6fabd22e0e04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331247/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "93b38f68-118d-558f-838d-db0afe705097" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-331247/selection/829b3930583e141a0ce52144501442c6c6de79c395438fc37ac1abec2dcd19ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-331247/selection/d0651a03f89efc45286c5ee45fac8bf8f6d7b9b0cd6a947e1d978a610d619c14" "q-331247/selection/d4411b0b8adc92f9440ec8b8964b562b4b02b35837ef02493727c43f1c036e13" "q-331247/selection/5c02fe71dfac7f98b265c90f1e44aeb5a5c279dcfb3ac6a58ac89e9bcb719224" "q-331247/selection/860efc4fc7ca904e76025a2ad9def958d3f156d5a46bfc9b4fb739fb4c332b9f" "q-331247/selection/829b3930583e141a0ce52144501442c6c6de79c395438fc37ac1abec2dcd19ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331247" :question/id #uuid "21b30c81-0ab1-5ce4-a724-5f5e1559092f" :question/math-academy-id "q-331247" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/d82e49514527a4f46f7bb212f0169806eb1475dd5324dda88ab3d733521540a9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $1$ unit to the right, giving $y=u(t-1).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331247/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f7a33f8-b0f6-5265-9d3e-c1ca34c07161"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331247"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331248/selection/f85edd83b1ed07e355b2f79f24c355ef2459f27cad57687fba9eb3dbfb8425a8" :answer/id #uuid "057cfba6-3f1a-562b-88cf-6ed7cb3f443e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t+3)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331248/selecti