n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $3$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+3).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t+3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331248/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f7a33f8-b0f6-5265-9d3e-c1ca34c07161"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331248"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-331185/selection/d0651a03f89efc45286c5ee45fac8bf8f6d7b9b0cd6a947e1d978a610d619c14" :answer/id #uuid "8af50beb-c843-54f4-8247-547147ee4e88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t+1)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331185/selection/6dfa55661a444290b6c293451e861900faf3bc4e5237387e879e0dc5d96c8198" :answer/id #uuid "9897b502-a26a-5ea2-b545-f024841ec582" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(t+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331185/selection/5c02fe71dfac7f98b265c90f1e44aeb5a5c279dcfb3ac6a58ac89e9bcb719224" :answer/id #uuid "ffbb67b0-cdb6-5a80-a77c-d557c3a1dad4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t-1)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331185/selection/f0ffc343e4dcdb580bd52e336e152df342875ca410bc7e91002f499380c4a82f" :answer/id #uuid "184e7c5f-3d47-5706-9076-adb0c3a1e6a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(t+2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331185/selection/860efc4fc7ca904e76025a2ad9def958d3f156d5a46bfc9b4fb739fb4c332b9f" :answer/id #uuid "5da9e907-aa4f-5f60-8463-5d39b6a89900" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331185/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "078e107b-7b29-5a1f-9685-66391c09a6b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-331185/selection/860efc4fc7ca904e76025a2ad9def958d3f156d5a46bfc9b4fb739fb4c332b9f" :answer-field/choices ["q-331185/selection/d0651a03f89efc45286c5ee45fac8bf8f6d7b9b0cd6a947e1d978a610d619c14" "q-331185/selection/6dfa55661a444290b6c293451e861900faf3bc4e5237387e879e0dc5d96c8198" "q-331185/selection/5c02fe71dfac7f98b265c90f1e44aeb5a5c279dcfb3ac6a58ac89e9bcb719224" "q-331185/selection/f0ffc343e4dcdb580bd52e336e152df342875ca410bc7e91002f499380c4a82f" "q-331185/selection/860efc4fc7ca904e76025a2ad9def958d3f156d5a46bfc9b4fb739fb4c332b9f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331185" :question/id #uuid "667d57dc-7eb9-57bc-b685-cfc00950512e" :question/math-academy-id "q-331185" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/6f80319af7f362c2a902853687670a9dab8e88778521147942dfb9a0d9872b75.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/4f7a1a32f5c639ec0a9433fc6d330e5ea9f8ed6fa1602441b54c9158bb69f63e.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by one unit to the right, giving $y=u(t-1).$\n\n- Then, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(t-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(t-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331185/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f7a33f8-b0f6-5265-9d3e-c1ca34c07161"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331185"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-331270"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/64854996af4b66e1095cbbcb94832593f1b76a8e9f0417ee37d187e83faff0eb.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we scale $y=u(-t)$ by a factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(-t).$\n\n- There is no horizontal shift or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(-t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-331268/selection/fc9dd2c81af77b48d24e66586fd3ad5454068a5d4eaa5db05b7828cbc841bad2" :answer/id #uuid "ec418e1d-c183-55a3-9342-34dbfcb2afea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(1-t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331268/selection/d4bef55b95d6be0ebea3302870391fb6670f953e5991d26085237096b1808024" :answer/id #uuid "da5d927c-cce5-5377-be18-3c88dc82cb4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(-1-t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331268/selection/e3777c93b27a279375a062654ba1d8cec6263b65868324b8078462a07aa04829" :answer/id #uuid "1d283bc8-5672-59df-b900-4253f13bdd91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(-1-t)+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331268/selection/d4411b0b8adc92f9440ec8b8964b562b4b02b35837ef02493727c43f1c036e13" :answer/id #uuid "bda81262-ad38-5d43-9163-98eb12f7321a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(t+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331268/selection/f912e99cd02f0b2adbfee9b8a92c8ee912a2c0945833aa4221d10ef469e3c20a" :answer/id #uuid "2203752b-9859-5c77-a198-cc559c376974" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(-1-t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-331268/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3cdeb90-e32a-5124-831a-a04dc706f455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-331268/selection/f912e99cd02f0b2adbfee9b8a92c8ee912a2c0945833aa4221d10ef469e3c20a" :answer-field/choices ["q-331268/selection/fc9dd2c81af77b48d24e66586fd3ad5454068a5d4eaa5db05b7828cbc841bad2" "q-331268/selection/d4bef55b95d6be0ebea3302870391fb6670f953e5991d26085237096b1808024" "q-331268/selection/e3777c93b27a279375a062654ba1d8cec6263b65868324b8078462a07aa04829" "q-331268/selection/d4411b0b8adc92f9440ec8b8964b562b4b02b35837ef02493727c43f1c036e13" "q-331268/selection/f912e99cd02f0b2adbfee9b8a92c8ee912a2c0945833aa4221d10ef469e3c20a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-331268" :question/id #uuid "af5ad942-27df-5509-b9a0-2deb2c68a6e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-331268" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/50c72e6334bdf9638b7915ef96157f871310faf6c9d3b7bfc4d4f8c7f65c9d26.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/a1748c6e23d99ad6ef43d5aceedcadb7a12fcdbef5064f93202d2fe5ca3d98d7.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y=u(-(t+1))=u(-1-t).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(-1-t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-331268/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "253b1011-a313-5004-8cbe-61133586e6b1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-331268"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-352909/selection/67679605518cfb468ad7a50299bb75a5f0b3bc3b008756bef4b2bdf44383cd9f" :answer/id #uuid "974099cb-bbb0-53a6-878a-78599615513f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2u(-t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352909/selection/1195a9d6d82189af6308bb03863a08c1508efd0518ef5ced98454f98a8dbb571" :answer/id #uuid "399df03a-7d3a-50a3-9e4d-7a1550dc5c28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(-t)+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352909/selection/716b3e01b1a02a8f6252f42aab77c713d8185a99cb30b120ce0c7b3420e2a665" :answer/id #uuid "5634c1c1-7806-5689-8f0d-3b3758b61d76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-u(-t)+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-352909/selection/b6d1ec99faebbcf24230857b2e227c0da60f78522faf990cfc610957a451d6dd" :answer/id #uuid "01c3e480-50fa-5b27-ae2e-79eb7fbb78df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "u(-t)-2" :db/ensure [:answer/vali