ference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/fe876b6075fa3e292595976c6d0077fec9c43b0af6a13c165abbd754fa7fcbe7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/385fe662259d5c933171b68f6b4b7227051921c3327da8f1ca10ff3bd6c5da3f.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y=u(-(t+1))=u(-t-1).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=4u(-t-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=4u(-t-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-352913/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "253b1011-a313-5004-8cbe-61133586e6b1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-352913"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21591"] :question/problem "Evaluate $f(t) = 9u(t-9)$ at $t=9,$ where $u$ is the unit step function." :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(9) & =9u(9-9) \\\\  & =9u(0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(9)=9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21592"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/fe9901de89c17bccdaa8c0f95440a079e6537dc42fe858fd836d89eb62f29ca7.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2).$\n\n- Then, we scale $y=u(t+2)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=3u(t+2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=3u(t+2).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21594"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/74bc7efebfaa6a798b848d9f0bbc400bc84a727a933fef32069fec6a87e6ed6b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y = u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14037461/assets/666fe470145e36e88a33d0719268c25226606d4de10e57f1a8e97ccfa4d34eaa.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y = u(t)$ to the given function $y = f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y = u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y = u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y = u(-(t+1)) = u(-1 - t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y = 2u(-1 - t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t) = 2u(-1 - t).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
